WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.742
[helicopter whirring]

00:00:01.742 --> 00:00:02.883
Nail that sucker!

00:00:02.883 --> 00:00:05.749
[gunfire]

00:00:05.749 --> 00:00:07.190
All right, first of all,
one of the great movies

00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:09.590
of all time, loved it when it came out.

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:12.340
But no. No, this is ridiculous.

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:13.880
Hi, my name is Chris Whitcomb.

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:15.710
I'm a former FBI agent.

00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:18.270
I was a member of the Hostage Rescue Team.

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:19.472
Been involved in dozens of

00:00:19.472 --> 00:00:21.413
high-profile operations around the world,

00:00:21.413 --> 00:00:23.855
including the siege at Ruby Ridge, Idaho,

00:00:23.855 --> 00:00:25.870
and at Waco, Texas.

00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:26.770
And today I'll be rating

00:00:26.770 --> 00:00:28.893
hostage-rescue scenes in movies.

00:00:33.830 --> 00:00:36.063
Pirate: You want a bullet in your head?

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:41.360
We do have to rappel from
time to time from above

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:44.020
down to the hostages to effect a rescue.

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:46.520
In this case, they use a
technique of Australian,

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:48.700
we used to call it Australian rappelling.

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:50.933
Oftentimes we'd use a breach,

00:00:50.933 --> 00:00:53.324
and we'd lower down an
explosive device below

00:00:53.324 --> 00:00:54.730
or something of that nature.

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:57.500
But in this case, they go face-first.

00:00:57.500 --> 00:00:59.433
The belay device is behind you,

00:00:59.433 --> 00:01:01.616
and you would use a hand behind your back

00:01:01.616 --> 00:01:03.000
so that you could face down.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.475
And it probably works better

00:01:04.475 --> 00:01:05.891
in this type of a situation.

00:01:05.891 --> 00:01:08.640
Anytime you have people
gathered in one area,

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:09.670
you could have hostages.

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:11.290
So we have to prepare
for all of those things.

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:12.148
You could have a plane,

00:01:12.148 --> 00:01:13.831
you could have a high-rise building,

00:01:13.831 --> 00:01:14.980
you could have any of those things.

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:17.690
A ship underway at sea

00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:19.060
has got to be one of the most difficult.

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:21.862
So, we practice hostage
rescue on ships, yes.

00:01:21.862 --> 00:01:23.430
You've got to get there to begin with,

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:24.880
which is a helicopter operation.

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:26.520
You've got to have some kind of stealth,

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:27.820
which means night vision.

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:30.400
You're probably above where
they're keeping the hostages,

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:32.270
and then you have to get down to them.

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:35.120
So that would entail fighting
your way down stairwells.

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:36.900
You could go down the outside of the ship

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:38.270
and go in from the outside.

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:40.253
Captain America: On my mark.

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:43.420
[gunfire]

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:48.050
Anytime you have a
hostage-rescue operation,

00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:49.620
you've got a lot of moving pieces.

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:52.080
You have to make sure they
all go at the same time.

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:54.730
You want to go in with speed, surprise,

00:01:54.730 --> 00:01:56.130
and violence of action.

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:58.879
The sniper shots, then the door breach,

00:01:58.879 --> 00:02:00.560
and then the entry by the assaulters.

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:01.910
So that's quite realistic.

00:02:01.910 --> 00:02:03.630
If you can get into a space

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:06.320
without sending people
inside, that's ideal.

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:09.075
So in this case, snipers executed

00:02:09.075 --> 00:02:10.920
what we call an open-air option,

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:13.930
which you send bullets into
the space, not human beings.

00:02:13.930 --> 00:02:15.660
And that was very well done in this scene.

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:17.720
Shooting is a very, very
important part of this job,

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:19.420
but it's only one element of the job.

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:22.050
The coordination is very, very important.

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:24.960
So we would practice that
day after day after day.

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:28.290
If you're a sniper, you
shoot at various distances,

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:30.470
but you practice that coordinated shot.

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:32.470
And it's almost like
being a drummer in a band,

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:33.920
where you just get used to the rhythm.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:35.560
In a training environment, you would hear,

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:39.240
"Ready, ready, ready, ready, aim, fire."

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:41.370
And you have to shoot on that cadence.

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:43.645
So it is very important

00:02:43.645 --> 00:02:45.703
that the sniper shots go together.

00:02:45.703 --> 00:02:47.630
Well, look, it's a superhero movie, right?

00:02:47.630 --> 00:02:49.200
And it's entertainment.

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:50.580
But I think they got
a lot of things right.

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:52.763
I'd have to give it a seven.

00:02:52.763 --> 00:02:54.270
Officer: I need eyes on.

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:56.360
Sir, they're gonna shoot him!

00:02:56.360 --> 00:02:57.351
Commander: Execute.

00:02:57.351 --> 00:02:58.776
[gunfire]

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:03.940
It's difficult to convey
how difficult this job is.

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:07.715
You have an unstable
platform shooting off a ship

00:03:07.715 --> 00:03:11.160
at another unstable platform,
another ship, at night,

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:15.240
through a tiny, tiny
opening in that lifeboat,

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:16.410
that small window.

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:19.810
Things happen so quickly, and
there is no room for error.

00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:21.790
It's an incredibly difficult shot,

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:23.790
and that's pretty accurate
the way it happens.

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:26.370
You get a very, very small window,

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:28.320
and the consequences are life and death.

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:30.195
You hear a lot about sniper pairs.

00:03:30.195 --> 00:03:32.237
The Hostage Rescue Team, when I was there,

00:03:32.237 --> 00:03:33.720
we would operate in pairs,

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:36.390
but not because one was an
observer and one was a shooter.

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:39.280
It's just so one can be on
a scope and ready to shoot

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:40.540
so the other can rest.

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:43.390
Oftentimes it is just because

00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:45.990
the ability to take a shot

00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:47.930
can erode very, very quickly

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:50.640
and you have to be fresh when
you need to take the shot.

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:52.370
If the shot needs to be taken,

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:55.490
it's usually because the
situation has eroded quickly

00:03:55.490 --> 00:03:58.590
and so badly that you
have no other option.

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:00.650
Look, this is based on a real-life event.

00:04:00.650 --> 00:04:03.270
It was entertainment and
built up a little bit,

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:05.000
but based on actual events,

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:06.773
so I've got to give it a nine.

00:04:06.773 --> 00:04:08.378
[helicopter whirring]

00:04:08.378 --> 00:04:09.793
Big Johnson: Nail that sucker!

00:04:09.793 --> 00:04:12.500
[gunfire]
[people screaming]

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:13.798
All right, first of all,
one of the great movies

00:04:13.798 --> 00:04:16.217
of all time, loved it when it came out.

00:04:16.217 --> 00:04:19.740
But no. No, this is ridiculous.

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:23.060
Hostage rescue is a very
carefully coordinated,

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:24.580
high-risk operation.

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:26.550
We did occasionally
look at the possibility

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:27.620
of shooting from a helicopter.

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:29.620
I've got a photograph
of me doing just that.

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:33.050
The problem is you wouldn't
fire an automatic weapon

00:04:33.050 --> 00:04:34.350
from a helicopter.

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:36.860
Certainly not with
civilians standing around.

00:04:36.860 --> 00:04:39.720
If anybody fired, it would
not be suppressive fire.

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:41.480
It would be a sniper shot.

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:43.380
The first thing I think
people need to understand

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:47.330
is that hostage rescue is
very, very highly refined.

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:49.150
They have hostage negotiators,

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:51.560
they have intelligence-gathering
mechanisms,

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:53.640
and the last resort is the application

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:54.850
of the Hostage Rescue Team.

00:04:54.850 --> 00:04:58.470
Very rarely would you
ever see a rapid action

00:04:58.470 --> 00:05:00.060
like depicted in this movie.

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:02.140
They're very, very carefully coordinated,

00:05:02.140 --> 00:05:04.480
and they work with precision.

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:06.460
None of those things
you see in this movie.

00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:07.851
I've got to rate the movie as a 10.

00:05:07.851 --> 00:05:09.000
Great entertainment.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:10.800
But as a depiction of a hostage rescue,

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:13.850
I'd give it a one.

00:05:13.850 --> 00:05:15.657
Where's Jerry? Jerry, watch the stairs.

00:05:15.657 --> 00:05:16.708
X-rays may escape down.

00:05:16.708 --> 00:05:18.100
There'll be shooting, so watch your ass.

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:19.390
Chris: Well, it's interesting.
When this happened,

00:05:19.390 --> 00:05:20.400
I was going to school.

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:22.210
I was in college in London,

00:05:22.210 --> 00:05:23.830
and I lived just around the corner.

00:05:23.830 --> 00:05:26.670
I was there and watched the raid.

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:28.220
One really great thing about this movie

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:30.070
in showing the history of this art form,

00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:33.900
is the, what we call a kill
house, a shooting house,

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:36.300
various names, but you have to practice

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:39.020
going into a space
shooting live ammunition,

00:05:39.020 --> 00:05:41.320
door breaches in low-light conditions

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:43.980
with all kinds of
terrible things going on,

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:47.650
explosions, people
screaming, smoke, everything,

00:05:47.650 --> 00:05:50.000
and they would create an indoor space,

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:51.750
practice over and over and over again

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:53.230
going through that space,

00:05:53.230 --> 00:05:56.250
and it makes the odds of
success that much better.

00:05:56.250 --> 00:05:58.330
You want a familiar environment,

00:05:58.330 --> 00:06:00.372
and that's what you're seeing here.

00:06:04.140 --> 00:06:05.640
Things always go wrong.

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:06.780
No matter how much you practice,

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:07.888
no matter how good you are,

00:06:07.888 --> 00:06:09.010
things always go wrong.

00:06:09.010 --> 00:06:11.160
Guys get hung up, and ropes break,

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:13.193
and explosives go off
or they don't go off.

00:06:13.193 --> 00:06:14.870
You have to make alternate breaches.

00:06:14.870 --> 00:06:16.615
And I think the movie depicted pretty well

00:06:16.615 --> 00:06:20.010
what went wrong and how
they rolled with that

00:06:20.010 --> 00:06:23.210
and how fluid a hostage rescue can be,

00:06:23.210 --> 00:06:25.810
because you can't stop
and do it over again.

00:06:25.810 --> 00:06:27.810
Once you roll, it's on.

00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:31.290
Oftentimes you'll have
an initial breach point,

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:33.560
but you'll have alternate
breach points as well.

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:34.700
You'll have alternate plans.

00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:36.670
People get shot, people go down.

00:06:36.670 --> 00:06:37.890
You have medical emergencies,

00:06:37.890 --> 00:06:40.750
you have a million different
things that can go wrong.

00:06:40.750 --> 00:06:43.400
So you have to improvise, adapt, overcome.

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:45.550
We always used to say, you
could teach anybody to shoot,

00:06:45.550 --> 00:06:47.330
you can get anybody physically fit;

00:06:47.330 --> 00:06:49.920
it's knowing when and
how to pull a trigger.

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:53.550
It's knowing when and how to
come up with a backup plan.

00:06:53.550 --> 00:06:54.710
Those things are really important.

00:06:54.710 --> 00:06:56.480
And that happened at Prince's Gate,

00:06:56.480 --> 00:06:58.330
and it's depicted well in this movie.

00:06:59.342 --> 00:07:01.783
Hostage taker: Move! Move, move!

00:07:02.850 --> 00:07:06.240
Chris: The SAS went in, and
some of the hostage takers

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:08.750
tried to sneak out with the hostages.

00:07:08.750 --> 00:07:10.770
Agent: We've got a room full of hostages.

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:11.636
Stay down!

00:07:11.636 --> 00:07:14.040
Chris: When you go in
with a tactical element

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:16.156
for a hostage-rescue operation,

00:07:16.156 --> 00:07:17.597
everybody goes to the floor.

00:07:17.597 --> 00:07:19.270
You don't know who's the bad guy.

00:07:19.270 --> 00:07:22.136
It's smoke, it's violent, it's chaos.

00:07:22.136 --> 00:07:23.420
It is absolute chaos.

00:07:23.420 --> 00:07:27.110
Except for the people
doing the rescue operation.

00:07:27.110 --> 00:07:30.377
This was a perfect place
where the British government

00:07:30.377 --> 00:07:32.280
realized how difficult that can be.

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:33.565
I think the most important thing

00:07:33.565 --> 00:07:34.700
to get out of some of these movies

00:07:34.700 --> 00:07:37.525
is the people, the
operators that do this job,

00:07:37.525 --> 00:07:39.633
they are truly remarkable.

00:07:39.633 --> 00:07:43.133
These are people who are more than willing

00:07:43.133 --> 00:07:45.366
to run in and die if that's the case,

00:07:45.366 --> 00:07:46.816
but don't plan to.

00:07:46.816 --> 00:07:49.005
I love this movie. I thought
they did a brilliant job.

00:07:49.005 --> 00:07:50.246
I'd rate it a 10.

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:54.163
Batman: Fox, the SWATs are
targeting the wrong people.

00:07:54.163 --> 00:07:55.740
The clowns are the hostages.

00:07:55.740 --> 00:07:57.750
Chris: Batman would help in any situation.

00:07:57.750 --> 00:07:58.930
I wish we had a Batman, right?

00:07:58.930 --> 00:08:00.740
Some elements of this
are really well done.

00:08:00.740 --> 00:08:02.610
Rappelling, I guess, was pretty accurate.

00:08:02.610 --> 00:08:04.593
The weapons look pretty good.

00:08:04.593 --> 00:08:06.026
The tactics looked bad.

00:08:06.026 --> 00:08:08.690
The lifeblood of any operation
like this is intelligence.

00:08:08.690 --> 00:08:10.950
You want information,
accurate information.

00:08:10.950 --> 00:08:14.590
So if they, being the command element,

00:08:14.590 --> 00:08:16.210
knew that hostages had weapons,

00:08:16.210 --> 00:08:17.860
we didn't know who was who,

00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:20.110
it would make it very, very
difficult to send somebody in.

00:08:20.110 --> 00:08:22.840
This is a good example
of when you don't go in.

00:08:22.840 --> 00:08:26.120
When you go into a building
like this, as I said before,

00:08:26.120 --> 00:08:27.960
you have to make sure everybody is secure,

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:29.610
because you don't know
who's good and who's bad

00:08:29.610 --> 00:08:30.730
in all circumstances.

00:08:30.730 --> 00:08:32.230
If somebody is shooting at you,

00:08:32.230 --> 00:08:33.690
obviously you're gonna shoot them.

00:08:33.690 --> 00:08:36.500
If they have a weapon and you
can take them to the ground,

00:08:36.500 --> 00:08:37.860
that might be a better option.

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:40.200
Some things are well done and plausible,

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:42.930
but it's, listen, it's a superhero movie.

00:08:42.930 --> 00:08:45.390
I don't know. Maybe a seven?

00:08:45.390 --> 00:08:47.333
Sniper: I've got an easy
shot, please advise.

00:08:47.333 --> 00:08:48.266
Radio: Stand by.

00:08:48.266 --> 00:08:49.433
Sniper: Roger that.

00:08:50.858 --> 00:08:53.492
[rat squeaks]
[sniper yelps and fires]

00:08:53.492 --> 00:08:54.490
Things go wrong.

00:08:54.490 --> 00:08:56.460
I one time was on an operation

00:08:56.460 --> 00:09:00.330
and we had this very, very,
very sensitive insertion.

00:09:00.330 --> 00:09:02.570
We couldn't get busted
under any circumstances.

00:09:02.570 --> 00:09:04.812
And one of the guys in my sniper cadre,

00:09:04.812 --> 00:09:07.820
he crawled off in a ground hornets' nest

00:09:07.820 --> 00:09:09.860
and just gets hammered

00:09:09.860 --> 00:09:12.235
in what might be a
life-threatening situation.

00:09:12.235 --> 00:09:14.785
Never made a sound, didn't flinch,

00:09:14.785 --> 00:09:16.890
and had to exfil for medical treatment.

00:09:16.890 --> 00:09:20.002
So things do go wrong,
but you have to eat them.

00:09:20.002 --> 00:09:21.620
There's no training for that.

00:09:21.620 --> 00:09:23.120
You've got to remember that these guys

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:24.780
are incredibly disciplined.

00:09:24.780 --> 00:09:26.650
A rat's not gonna bother anybody, no.

00:09:26.650 --> 00:09:28.170
Tubular targets are difficult,

00:09:28.170 --> 00:09:29.380
and they're oftentimes moving.

00:09:29.380 --> 00:09:33.650
You got a car, a bus, a
plane, a train, a subway car,

00:09:33.650 --> 00:09:36.760
and even if he hadn't
responded to that rat

00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:38.493
and had taken a stable shot,

00:09:38.493 --> 00:09:41.009
a bullet going through a medium like glass

00:09:41.009 --> 00:09:42.610
can do a million different things.

00:09:42.610 --> 00:09:44.080
So you have to practice that too.

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:47.040
Not just the shot, but
what the bullet's gonna do

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:48.950
in so many different circumstances.

00:09:48.950 --> 00:09:50.630
You don't want to shoot through a target,

00:09:50.630 --> 00:09:53.533
put somebody else on the other
side in danger, obviously.

00:09:53.533 --> 00:09:56.970
[gunfire]

00:09:56.970 --> 00:09:58.658
But one thing that's really
important to remember

00:09:58.658 --> 00:10:00.810
is that you always have a backup plan.

00:10:00.810 --> 00:10:02.885
So if you have a sniper shot,

00:10:02.885 --> 00:10:04.785
it is going to be followed up

00:10:04.785 --> 00:10:06.820
with an assault component as well.

00:10:06.820 --> 00:10:09.620
And if that assault
component is ready to go,

00:10:09.620 --> 00:10:12.100
they would have stormed
the train immediately

00:10:12.100 --> 00:10:13.460
and resolved it in a different way.

00:10:13.460 --> 00:10:16.780
So the planning and the
execution of this raid

00:10:16.780 --> 00:10:18.410
would not have gone this way,

00:10:18.410 --> 00:10:21.850
because we would have
some immediate action,

00:10:21.850 --> 00:10:24.110
an emergency assault if necessary,

00:10:24.110 --> 00:10:26.240
had a shot gone wrong for whatever reason.

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:27.330
And I think the scene makes sense

00:10:27.330 --> 00:10:28.680
in a lot of different ways,

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:30.238
but a rat crawling up a sniper's leg

00:10:30.238 --> 00:10:32.995
is not going to have this impact, no.

00:10:36.590 --> 00:10:38.340
Well, I've gotta say, I'm
kind of partial to this movie.

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:40.520
First of all, the director, Peter Berg,

00:10:40.520 --> 00:10:43.370
is a very good friend of mine for 35 years

00:10:43.370 --> 00:10:44.380
or something like that.

00:10:44.380 --> 00:10:47.470
He put me in the movie
kind of as a cameo thing,

00:10:47.470 --> 00:10:49.387
like, play the commander of that

00:10:49.387 --> 00:10:51.623
hostage-rescue-team component right there.

00:10:56.090 --> 00:10:57.880
He's putting on a door breach right now.

00:10:57.880 --> 00:10:59.290
And this is a slap charge,

00:10:59.290 --> 00:11:01.470
which is basically a piece of rubber

00:11:01.470 --> 00:11:03.910
with some plastic explosive.

00:11:03.910 --> 00:11:05.580
You have the overall Hostage Rescue Team,

00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:07.540
then you have smaller components.

00:11:07.540 --> 00:11:10.790
At that time, the teams were
about seven men on a team,

00:11:10.790 --> 00:11:12.677
and that's what you see.

00:11:12.677 --> 00:11:13.510
[door explodes]

00:11:13.510 --> 00:11:14.356
Go, go, go, go!

00:11:14.356 --> 00:11:15.189
Get out, get out!

00:11:15.189 --> 00:11:16.590
Chris: Anybody can blow open a door.

00:11:16.590 --> 00:11:17.790
You wanna blow open the door

00:11:17.790 --> 00:11:20.350
so it doesn't bounce off the backstop.

00:11:20.350 --> 00:11:21.663
So there's an art to

00:11:21.663 --> 00:11:23.952
the explosive breaching
in these enterprises,

00:11:23.952 --> 00:11:25.580
and that's what we're showing right here.

00:11:25.580 --> 00:11:28.580
HRT went up to try and assist everybody

00:11:28.580 --> 00:11:30.190
in the Boston operation.

00:11:30.190 --> 00:11:32.610
And when they got there,
there were so many cops

00:11:32.610 --> 00:11:33.670
and so many people involved,

00:11:33.670 --> 00:11:35.890
they couldn't get any rental cars.

00:11:35.890 --> 00:11:38.340
So they flew up and they
had to rent minivans.

00:11:38.340 --> 00:11:42.620
So the HRT operation for
the Boston Marathon bombing

00:11:42.620 --> 00:11:44.160
that you see depicted in the movie,

00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:46.450
the guys actually arrived in minivans.

00:11:46.450 --> 00:11:50.240
So you do what you have
to do from time to time.

00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:51.132
I just want to point out,

00:11:51.132 --> 00:11:53.180
the weapons you use in
these types of situations,

00:11:53.180 --> 00:11:54.980
I always looked at guns as tools.

00:11:54.980 --> 00:11:57.190
A carpenter can't build a
house with a screwdriver,

00:11:57.190 --> 00:11:58.073
so you have different weapons.

00:11:58.073 --> 00:11:59.780
And what you see right here

00:11:59.780 --> 00:12:02.230
is a typical assault rifle.

00:12:02.230 --> 00:12:04.360
I personally had 17 different weapons

00:12:04.360 --> 00:12:05.420
for different situations.

00:12:05.420 --> 00:12:07.400
Everything from a .50 caliber,

00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:10.116
a long gun for sniper applications,

00:12:10.116 --> 00:12:11.566
to something like this.

00:12:11.566 --> 00:12:14.070
This might be used as a door breach.

00:12:14.070 --> 00:12:15.720
It's not your primary weapon,

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:17.170
but you might need to go to it.

00:12:17.170 --> 00:12:18.940
So your primary weapon might be

00:12:18.940 --> 00:12:21.600
something small and compact like this.

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:24.150
If that weapon goes down,
for whatever reason,

00:12:24.150 --> 00:12:25.630
you could go with a backup weapon,

00:12:25.630 --> 00:12:27.690
which would be some handgun,

00:12:27.690 --> 00:12:31.060
this is a 9 millimeter, which
oftentimes we would use.

00:12:31.060 --> 00:12:32.670
In a case like this, you might carry

00:12:32.670 --> 00:12:34.040
three or four different weapons.

00:12:34.040 --> 00:12:37.020
What you use for weapons
really can make a difference,

00:12:37.020 --> 00:12:39.150
especially in closed spaces like this.

00:12:39.150 --> 00:12:40.270
Well, obviously I'm prejudiced,

00:12:40.270 --> 00:12:42.370
but Pete Berg did a brilliant job here.

00:12:42.370 --> 00:12:45.360
And although a very,
very simple operation,

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:46.850
this is the way it would
go down in real life,

00:12:46.850 --> 00:12:47.993
the way it did go down in real life,

00:12:47.993 --> 00:12:50.410
so I've got to give it a 10.

00:12:50.410 --> 00:12:52.310
Got it.

00:12:52.310 --> 00:12:53.510
She'll miss.

00:12:53.510 --> 00:12:55.410
What's the probability of that?

00:12:55.410 --> 00:12:58.038
Based on the temperature and humidity,

00:12:58.038 --> 00:13:00.963
no wind, half moon, good light,

00:13:00.963 --> 00:13:02.621
97.6%.

00:13:02.621 --> 00:13:05.010
Chris: A shot from any
gun is simple physics.

00:13:05.010 --> 00:13:06.610
You got a pellet going through the air.

00:13:06.610 --> 00:13:08.190
It's pulled out by gravity,

00:13:08.190 --> 00:13:10.720
and it's impacted in its trajectory.

00:13:10.720 --> 00:13:13.600
A short shot has fewer variables.

00:13:13.600 --> 00:13:15.510
A long shot has greater variables.

00:13:15.510 --> 00:13:18.740
In this case, you're talking
about a handgun at, what,

00:13:18.740 --> 00:13:20.580
maybe 20 feet, something like that.

00:13:20.580 --> 00:13:22.670
Humidity, light, all of those things

00:13:22.670 --> 00:13:24.440
are not gonna have much of an impact.

00:13:24.440 --> 00:13:26.940
A longer shot, it's
gonna have a huge impact.

00:13:26.940 --> 00:13:29.131
Move, or I'll do it.

00:13:29.131 --> 00:13:29.964
Take it.

00:13:29.964 --> 00:13:31.205
[gunshot]

00:13:32.097 --> 00:13:33.310
- Mom?
- Oh, baby.

00:13:33.310 --> 00:13:35.840
A sniper in HRT one time
took a shot at a guy

00:13:35.840 --> 00:13:38.650
who had a child taped to his back

00:13:38.650 --> 00:13:42.430
and had a woman in front of
him with a shotgun to her head.

00:13:42.430 --> 00:13:43.621
So the child's head,

00:13:43.621 --> 00:13:45.487
I think it was almost touching his,

00:13:45.487 --> 00:13:49.050
and an HRT sniper had to take that shot,

00:13:49.050 --> 00:13:50.650
moving from left to right.

00:13:50.650 --> 00:13:53.800
It is difficult to explain to people

00:13:53.800 --> 00:13:57.900
how difficult it is to
shoot another human being

00:13:57.900 --> 00:14:00.710
under all of the various circumstances.

00:14:00.710 --> 00:14:03.450
I'm just going to say
that it is plausible.

00:14:03.450 --> 00:14:05.340
The dialogue was reasonably well done.

00:14:05.340 --> 00:14:06.660
They're things that I
would have thought about,

00:14:06.660 --> 00:14:09.060
so I'd probably give it an eight, I think.

00:14:09.060 --> 00:14:11.905
Agent: The sniper observers
will secure the perimeter.

00:14:13.243 --> 00:14:14.600
Chris: I don't know what that building is.

00:14:14.600 --> 00:14:16.820
That wasn't at Ruby Ridge.
I don't know what that is.

00:14:16.820 --> 00:14:19.260
Agent: We'll have the gold team

00:14:19.260 --> 00:14:21.210
backing up the blue team position.

00:14:21.210 --> 00:14:22.840
Chris: It's infuriating to me.

00:14:22.840 --> 00:14:23.890
I was one of those guys.

00:14:23.890 --> 00:14:25.960
There were seven of us,
not a roomful of us.

00:14:25.960 --> 00:14:28.500
We were standing in ghillie
suits in the pouring rain.

00:14:28.500 --> 00:14:30.510
It was 32 degrees or something like that.

00:14:30.510 --> 00:14:32.140
The rules of engagement
were a little bit different.

00:14:32.140 --> 00:14:34.150
I testified before the Senate,

00:14:34.150 --> 00:14:36.430
we've had all kinds of
investigations over the years,

00:14:36.430 --> 00:14:38.890
and we were going up under
totally different circumstances.

00:14:38.890 --> 00:14:41.220
So there's nothing even remotely realistic

00:14:41.220 --> 00:14:42.800
about what you just saw.

00:14:42.800 --> 00:14:43.733
Bet they're out there.

00:14:43.733 --> 00:14:45.108
I guess they don't wanna talk.

00:14:51.340 --> 00:14:53.787
Well, there were seven of
us up there on that hill.

00:14:53.787 --> 00:14:55.900
I was lying behind a sniper rifle,

00:14:55.900 --> 00:14:57.810
had the whole thing in my scope,

00:14:57.810 --> 00:15:00.750
I watched everything
from beginning to end,

00:15:00.750 --> 00:15:03.870
and I can tell you that
is the most ridiculous,

00:15:03.870 --> 00:15:05.610
false representation of what happened

00:15:05.610 --> 00:15:06.750
I could possibly state.

00:15:06.750 --> 00:15:09.820
The fact that a sniper would
be standing up behind a tree,

00:15:09.820 --> 00:15:11.070
which is absurd.

00:15:11.070 --> 00:15:13.408
The only thing that's accurate
about that entire clip,

00:15:13.408 --> 00:15:15.810
Lon Horiuchi's shot did
go through the door,

00:15:15.810 --> 00:15:19.250
the pane of glass in the
door, through a curtain,

00:15:19.250 --> 00:15:21.010
and the door looked similar to that door.

00:15:21.010 --> 00:15:22.780
So the curtain and the door look similar.

00:15:22.780 --> 00:15:26.620
The circumstances are
not remotely accurate.

00:15:26.620 --> 00:15:28.240
They didn't come up
and have a conversation

00:15:28.240 --> 00:15:29.930
standing on a rock.

00:15:29.930 --> 00:15:31.110
None of that happened.

00:15:31.110 --> 00:15:33.070
It is complete and utter fiction.

00:15:33.070 --> 00:15:36.410
Bottom line is we were at a holding area

00:15:36.410 --> 00:15:38.690
in a valley down below the ridge.

00:15:38.690 --> 00:15:41.340
We land-navigated our way up through

00:15:41.340 --> 00:15:43.080
appalling weather conditions,

00:15:43.080 --> 00:15:44.730
and we set up a perimeter,

00:15:44.730 --> 00:15:47.813
a partial perimeter around the cabin.

00:15:47.813 --> 00:15:50.410
And I had just laid down
behind my sniper rifle,

00:15:50.410 --> 00:15:53.720
and I was just trying to get
an idea of what was going on.

00:15:53.720 --> 00:15:55.790
And our job was to send information

00:15:55.790 --> 00:15:57.270
back to the command element,

00:15:57.270 --> 00:15:59.120
because we didn't know
what was going on up there.

00:15:59.120 --> 00:16:01.245
It happened very, very quickly.

00:16:01.245 --> 00:16:02.470
Way below zero.

00:16:02.470 --> 00:16:05.318
It's just, it makes me so angry

00:16:05.318 --> 00:16:06.884
to think that people would watch that

00:16:06.884 --> 00:16:09.080
and think that happened. It's absurd.

00:16:09.080 --> 00:16:11.380
Negotiator: The siege is over now!

00:16:11.380 --> 00:16:13.080
The gas you will be smelling

00:16:13.080 --> 00:16:15.140
is a nonlethal tear gas.

00:16:15.140 --> 00:16:18.330
Chris: I was, I think, one of
the first two or three people

00:16:18.330 --> 00:16:19.580
that arrived at Waco.

00:16:19.580 --> 00:16:21.010
The only thing about this movie I like

00:16:21.010 --> 00:16:23.490
is it's incredible how accurately

00:16:23.490 --> 00:16:24.890
they recreated that building.

00:16:24.890 --> 00:16:26.260
I mean, absolutely unbelievable.

00:16:26.260 --> 00:16:28.660
So I'm not gonna talk
about what they did right

00:16:28.660 --> 00:16:30.320
and what they did wrong.

00:16:30.320 --> 00:16:31.570
I'm going to say that

00:16:31.570 --> 00:16:36.560
it was a very, very violent resolution,

00:16:36.560 --> 00:16:39.967
and it went terribly wrong
for all parties involved.

00:16:39.967 --> 00:16:43.196
[gunfire]

00:16:43.196 --> 00:16:44.980
[children crying]

00:16:44.980 --> 00:16:46.242
In this particular case,

00:16:46.242 --> 00:16:49.320
they sent us in to try
and get the kids out.

00:16:49.320 --> 00:16:50.930
We considered the children

00:16:50.930 --> 00:16:52.870
and the unwilling Branch Davidians

00:16:52.870 --> 00:16:54.540
inside the building as hostages,

00:16:54.540 --> 00:16:56.710
and our job was to get them out alive.

00:16:56.710 --> 00:16:58.750
We thought it would have made great sense

00:16:58.750 --> 00:17:00.750
if we'd gone in that night,

00:17:00.750 --> 00:17:04.130
doing what we as a Hostage
Rescue Team did very, very well.

00:17:04.130 --> 00:17:06.030
And we thought we could
have saved everybody.

00:17:06.030 --> 00:17:08.270
They'd been in a firefight,
they were wounded,

00:17:08.270 --> 00:17:09.480
they were on their heels.

00:17:09.480 --> 00:17:11.030
We thought we could have resolved it.

00:17:11.030 --> 00:17:12.410
That did not happen.

00:17:12.410 --> 00:17:14.440
What happened is we put a fence

00:17:14.440 --> 00:17:15.950
around the place, essentially,

00:17:15.950 --> 00:17:18.505
and waited until that last day

00:17:18.505 --> 00:17:21.030
when the attorney general
decided to send us in.

00:17:21.030 --> 00:17:24.435
By that time, the odds were
so heavily stacked against us,

00:17:24.435 --> 00:17:26.570
I don't know that anybody
ever really thought

00:17:26.570 --> 00:17:28.200
we had much of a chance.

00:17:28.200 --> 00:17:30.500
But obviously it didn't
go well then either.

00:17:37.670 --> 00:17:40.537
Once Washington, DC, made
the decision to go in,

00:17:40.537 --> 00:17:43.162
the plan was to insert

00:17:43.162 --> 00:17:45.712
a chemical irritant,

00:17:45.712 --> 00:17:48.250
tear gas, for lack of a better
term, into the building.

00:17:48.250 --> 00:17:50.232
So we had what looked like tanks,

00:17:50.232 --> 00:17:52.640
they were tanks, that
had nozzles on the front.

00:17:52.640 --> 00:17:54.510
In order to get the gas in the building,

00:17:54.510 --> 00:17:55.910
you have to inject it in the building.

00:17:55.910 --> 00:17:59.400
So the tanks drove up,
injected gas into the building.

00:17:59.400 --> 00:18:03.880
So we went with armored
vehicles, inserted the gas,

00:18:03.880 --> 00:18:05.505
tore some of the walls open

00:18:05.505 --> 00:18:07.210
to allow people to get out,

00:18:07.210 --> 00:18:09.600
and it went precipitously
downhill from there.

00:18:09.600 --> 00:18:13.510
And I think this was a
horrible misrepresentation

00:18:13.510 --> 00:18:14.480
of what happened.

00:18:14.480 --> 00:18:17.230
It may have represented
what the negotiators did,

00:18:17.230 --> 00:18:19.990
but it had little to do with the reality

00:18:19.990 --> 00:18:21.630
of what I experienced when I was there.

00:18:21.630 --> 00:18:23.470
And I thought it was appallingly bad.

00:18:23.470 --> 00:18:25.860
Ultimately, we want to
save people's lives,

00:18:25.860 --> 00:18:30.501
and this movie did not
accurately depict that

00:18:30.501 --> 00:18:31.826
in any way.

00:18:31.826 --> 00:18:33.130
Well, this is a personal thing for me,

00:18:33.130 --> 00:18:34.480
but I would rate it a zero.

00:18:36.630 --> 00:18:37.840
It makes me ill.

00:18:37.840 --> 00:18:40.270
Hostage rescue's not
really just guys going in

00:18:40.270 --> 00:18:42.460
through doors with machine
guns and black outfits on.

00:18:42.460 --> 00:18:45.090
It's really about an entire process.

00:18:45.090 --> 00:18:47.510
And the end result,
when nothing else works,

00:18:47.510 --> 00:18:49.700
is to send people through a door breach.

00:18:49.700 --> 00:18:51.010
You want to avoid that if possible.

00:18:51.010 --> 00:18:53.850
If you need to do that,
you need to be prepared

00:18:53.850 --> 00:18:55.823
and understand the consequences.

